<header class="page-head">
  <p class="page-kicker">{% set iconName = "results" %}{% include "partials/section-icon.njk" %} Records</p>
  <h1>F1 records: pole won 14 of the first 20 races in 2026</h1>
  <p class="dek">{{ summary }}</p>
  <p class="last-updated">Last checked <time datetime="{{ records.checkedOn }}">{{ records.checkedOn | displayDate }}</time></p>
</header>

<p>The polesitter won 14 of the 20 Grands Prix that had a race classification on the <a href="https://www.formula1.com/en/results/2026/races">Formula 1 2026 results index</a> when it was read on 21 November 2026. That is 70 percent. The same count was 16 of 24 in 2025 and 12 of 24 in 2024. <a href="https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/f1s-youngest-world-champions-as-antonelli-smashes-record.4otUr4h4wmWd6VmIEaNjzV">Formula 1's youngest champions article</a>, published 3 November 2026 and read again on 21 November, puts Kimi Antonelli first at 20 years and 68 days.</p>

<p>This page is a table of those published figures, plus the US circuit numbers Formula 1 prints on its circuit guides. It is not a live timing feed. Grid and Pit counted the pole to win column. Every other cell is copied from the linked page.</p>

<section class="dash-section" aria-labelledby="youngest-heading">
  <h2 id="youngest-heading">Youngest champions</h2>
  <p>Formula 1 published five names. The <a href="https://www.fia.com/news/f1-antonelli-crowned-f1s-youngest-world-champion-verstappen-wins-mexico-city">FIA race report</a> of 2 November 2026 calls Antonelli the youngest world champion and says he was 20, after third place in Mexico City. It does not print the day count. The ages below are the ones Formula 1 printed, not a count this desk worked out from birth dates.</p>
  <table class="dash-table">
    <caption>Five youngest first titles, as Formula 1 listed them on 3 November 2026.</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Rank</th>
        <th scope="col">Driver</th>
        <th scope="col">First title</th>
        <th scope="col">Age</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in records.youngest %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.rank }}</td>
        <th scope="row">{{ row.driver }}</th>
        <td>{{ row.season }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.years }} years and {{ row.days }} days</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A longer list sits on <a href="{{ '/articles/2026-10-28-youngest-f1-champions-antonelli/' | url }}">youngest F1 world champions</a>. That story follows a Wikipedia table checked on 20 November 2026, which prints Sebastian Vettel at 23 years and 134 days and Fernando Alonso at 24 years and 58 days. Formula 1 prints 133 and 57. This page does not average the two. The <a href="{{ '/articles/2026-10-27-f1-tiebreak-rules-explained/' | url }}">tiebreak explainer</a> is how a title is settled when the points are level, and the <a href="{{ '/articles/2026-11-02-mexico-city-gp-results-verstappen-wins-antonelli-champion/' | url }}">Mexico City result</a> is the race where Antonelli took the 2026 title.</p>
</section>

<section class="dash-section" aria-labelledby="pole-heading">
  <h2 id="pole-heading">Pole to win, season by season</h2>
  <p>Pole here means position 1 on the qualifying classification. The winner is position 1 on the race classification. A row counts only when those two names match. It is not the starting grid after a penalty, and a polesitter who does not win the race is a no. Las Vegas, Qatar and Abu Dhabi were linked on the 2026 index and had no winner in the classification, so they are not in the 20.</p>
  <table class="dash-table">
    <caption>Pole to win counts from the Formula 1 season results pages, read 21 November 2026.</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Season</th>
        <th scope="col">Races counted</th>
        <th scope="col">Won from pole</th>
        <th scope="col">Share</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for season in records.seasons %}
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">{{ season.season }}</th>
        <td>{{ season.races }}</td>
        <td>{{ season.poleWins }}</td>
        <td>{% if season.season == 2024 %}half, 12 of 24{% elif season.season == 2025 %}16 of 24, which is two thirds{% else %}14 of 20, which is 70 percent. The season is not finished.{% endif %}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The 2024 and 2025 race rows are in the CSV, in the order of each season's results index. The date in that file is the last day of the weekend range printed on the race result page. For 2026, the date is the Date column on the season index. The <a href="{{ '/articles/2026-11-03-f1-points-system-explained/' | url }}">points system</a> is a separate scale: a win pays 25 in a full Grand Prix whether or not it started from pole.</p>
</section>

